<!-- Merger trees used to test the satellite granule operator for soliton+NFW, soliton-only, and NFW-only host halos.

     The soliton status of a halo can not be specified here: it is a meta-property, which the tree builder does not read, and it is
     determined by the dark matter profile. Each host is therefore constructed such that it reaches the intended state:

      * a 10^10 Msun halo with a typical scale radius has a soliton+NFW structure;
      * a 10^8 Msun satellite which has already lost most of its mass, orbiting in the NFW part of a 10^10 Msun host, is tidally
        stripped down to its soliton immediately, becoming soliton-only, and so acts as a soliton-only host to a satellite of its
        own;
      * a 10^11 Msun halo with a very small scale radius (a concentration of about 7000) admits no soliton+NFW solution, and so is
        NFW-only.

     Satellite positions are chosen relative to the core and soliton radii of their hosts (for the random number seed used in
     testSuite/parameters/satelliteGranule.xml), and the model is evolved for a small fraction of an orbital period, so that each
     satellite remains in the same radial region throughout. testSuite/test-satellite-granule.py checks the state of each host,
     and which of the core and outer granule contributions act on each satellite. -->
<trees>

<!-- Soliton+NFW host (core radius 0.44 kpc, soliton radius 1.34 kpc), with satellites inside the core radius, between the core and
     soliton radii, and outside the soliton radius. -->
